Title:
lethe_amsi_veh: Bypassing AMSI Without Patching amsi.dll (Part 3)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post describes a native-shellcode AMSI bypass that avoids patching `amsi.dll` by using a Vectored Exception Handler (VEH) plus a hardware execute breakpoint on `AmsiScanBuffer`. It targets a timing problem in “ghost_loader” style .NET loading where AMSI scanning occurs during `Assembly.Load`/`AppDomain.Load_3` before any managed code can run to apply typical AMSI patches. The technique sets DR0/DR7 for the current thread (via `NtGetContextThread`/`NtSetContextThread`) so execution at `AmsiScanBuffer` triggers `EXCEPTION_SINGLE_STEP`, letting the handler force `AMSI_RESULT_CLEAN` and return `S_OK` without executing the function body. It also explains how to locate the `AMSI_RESULT*` out-parameter at `RSP+0x30` using the Windows x64 calling convention (shadow space + stack arguments) at the exact moment the breakpoint fires. The approach is positioned as less detectable by memory scanners because it doesn’t modify mapped module pages, and it’s scoped to the loader thread (hardware breakpoints are per-thread). Useful for red team operators, implant/loader developers, and defenders studying AMSI/EDR evasion tradecraft.

Technical Focus:
- AMSI interception at `AmsiScanBuffer` without inline patching
- Vectored Exception Handling (VEH) and `EXCEPTION_SINGLE_STEP`
- x64 hardware breakpoints (DR0/DR7) and per-thread debug registers
- Windows x64 calling convention (shadow space, stack argument layout)
- PIC/shellcode constraints and hash-based API resolution (DJB2)
- CLR/.NET load pipeline timing (`AppDomain.Load_3` vs `Invoke_3`)

Use Cases:
- Bypass AMSI scanning for in-memory .NET assembly loading when managed hooks run too late
- Implement stealthier AMSI evasion that avoids modifying `amsi.dll` code sections
- Build shellcode-based loaders that must remain stringless and position-independent
- Defensive research: create detections around debug-register usage + VEH registration patterns

Keywords:
AMSI, AmsiScanBuffer, amsi.dll, Vectored Exception Handler, VEH, EXCEPTION_SINGLE_STEP, hardware breakpoint, DR0, DR7, NtGetContextThread, NtSetContextThread, AddVectoredExceptionHandler, Windows x64 calling convention, shadow space, RIP/RSP manipulation, Assembly.Load, AppDomain.Load_3, shellcode, PIC, DJB2 hash API resolution